(A) Proposition 5.2(a), exact over all 2^16 functions f (uniform law on 16 inputs, fair coin, 24 tests of equal weight):
    eta = 0.5: bound sqrt(coll ln(2/(pi eta))/2) = 0.3777; fraction of functions with some test beyond the bound = 0.003632 (must be <= eta); largest deviation over all f = 0.5000
    eta = 0.1: bound sqrt(coll ln(2/(pi eta))/2) = 0.4392; fraction of functions with some test beyond the bound = 0.000214 (must be <= eta); largest deviation over all f = 0.5000
    eta = 0.01: bound sqrt(coll ln(2/(pi eta))/2) = 0.5147; fraction of functions with some test beyond the bound = 0.000000 (must be <= eta); largest deviation over all f = 0.5000
    TV(Q_f, Q_coin) = 1/2 for every f (part (b)); with only 16 inputs the bound is weak, the check is of the probability statement.
(B) Human = a random function F, imitator = the coin: probability (over F and N recorded runs) that the coin is NOT in the matching set M_N
    (kl form, level delta), exact; it is at most [the same probability for data from the coin, <= delta] + Pr[two recorded inputs coincide]:
    u = 6, N = 3, delta = 0.5: Pr[coin not in M_N | human = F] = 0.02778; Pr[coin not in M_N | human = coin] = 0.00694; Pr[collision] = 0.44444; sum = 0.45139
    u = 8, N = 4, delta = 0.5: Pr[coin not in M_N | human = F] = 0.05664; Pr[coin not in M_N | human = coin] = 0.01489; Pr[collision] = 0.58984; sum = 0.60474
    u = 8, N = 4, delta = 0.2: Pr[coin not in M_N | human = F] = 0.05664; Pr[coin not in M_N | human = coin] = 0.01489; Pr[collision] = 0.58984; sum = 0.60474
    u = 10, N = 5, delta = 0.5: Pr[coin not in M_N | human = F] = 0.08560; Pr[coin not in M_N | human = coin] = 0.02316; Pr[collision] = 0.69760; sum = 0.72076
(C) Example 5.3 (the same question twice; human = coin; imitator = a function g on 16 inputs), all 2^16 functions g, tests of (A):
    fraction of g with all per-call frequencies within 0.3777 of the human's: 0.9964 (at least 1/2 by Proposition 5.2(a) with eta = 1/2);
    for every g the run test 'the two answers agree' has probability 1 (imitator) against 1/2 (human system): difference 1/2.
(D) V1(b) with runs of variable length (L = 2, E[len] = 3/2): Delta(B) = 9/16 = 0.5625;
    mean of the recorded difference over ALL m rounds (empty rounds count 0) = 0.5625;  over the non-empty rounds only = 0.7500 (= Delta x L/E[len])
(E) Theorem 6.2(c), exact (5 inputs, 3 answers, a human P0 that is not uniform, 3 adaptive questions):
    150 procedures without repeated questions: E_F Pr_F[accept] = Pr_P0[accept] exactly: True;  150 procedures that may repeat: |difference| <= xi: True (largest difference 0.4056)
    one question 'ask input 1, accept iff the answer is 0': Pr_coin[accept] = 1/2; Pr_f[accept] is 0 or 1 for every function f:
    for each fixed f this procedure tells P_f from the coin with advantage 1/2; only the AVERAGE over f equals 1/2 (Theorem 6.2(c)), and (d) is one-sided.
    two questions 'ask input 1 twice, accept iff the two answers are equal': Pr_coin[accept] = 1/2, Pr_f[accept] = 1 for every f (here xi = 1).
    with 50 inputs asked twice each: the coin gives 50 equal pairs with probability 2^-50 = 8.88e-16; every function gives them with probability 1.
    The statistic is the frequency of the test 'the two answers agree' of a derived run that puts one question twice; it is not a frequency of a test of single pairs.
(E2) Two answers y1, y2 of the human to ONE input x, imitator's law A(.|x) known:
    binary answers: E[(y1 - a)(y2 - a)] = (p - a)^2 for all p, a in {0, 0.1, ..., 1}: True  (a = A(1|x), p = P(1|x))
    four answers:   E[1(y1 = y2) - A(y1|x) - A(y2|x) + sum_y A(y|x)^2] = sum_y (P(y|x) - A(y|x))^2 on 300 random pairs of laws: True
    imitator = coin: the statistic is 1/4 with certainty if the human is ANY function, and has mean 0 if the human is the coin.
    25 inputs asked twice (50 answers): Pr[the average statistic of a coin human reaches 1/8] <= 4.39e-02; a function human gives 1/4 always.
    100 inputs asked twice (200 answers): Pr[the average statistic of a coin human reaches 1/8] <= 3.73e-06; a function human gives 1/4 always.
    400 inputs asked twice (800 answers): Pr[the average statistic of a coin human reaches 1/8] <= 1.93e-22; a function human gives 1/4 always.
    So for binary answers, m probe inputs asked twice (2m answers) certify E_x (P(1|x) - A(1|x))^2 on the probe distribution to within
    sqrt(2 ln(2/delta)/m) (Hoeffding, terms in [-1, 1]), and the mean total variation per call is at most the square root of that number.
(F) Repetitions allowed, answer set of size s at each input: P0 uniform on s answers; P_F uniform on a random half of them (TV = 1/2 at every input).
    r answers to ONE input: total variation between the two laws of the r answers (exact; the law depends only on the pattern of coincidences):
    s =     16, r =   2: TV = 0.06250   (r^2/s = 0.2500)
    s =    256, r =   2: TV = 0.00391   (r^2/s = 0.0156)
    s =    256, r =   8: TV = 0.09543   (r^2/s = 0.2500)
    s =   4096, r =   8: TV = 0.00678   (r^2/s = 0.0156)
    s =   4096, r =  32: TV = 0.10175   (r^2/s = 0.2500)
    s =  65536, r =  32: TV = 0.00749   (r^2/s = 0.0156)
(G) Example 6.3 (d = 5): parity and the coin have exactly equal frequencies on 300 random tests that ignore one input bit: True;
    but the single question 'ask any x, accept iff y = parity(x)' accepts the parity human with probability 1 and the coin with probability 1/2:
    Example 6.3 is an example of equal test frequencies, not of two humans that m answers cannot tell apart.
